</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To compare with methods designed for single-step tasks, we evaluate simple tasks drawn from the out-of-distribution benchmarks used in the VLA papers, as shown in Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="TABLE IV ‣ IV-C Simple Task Evaluation ‣ IV EXPERIMENT ‣ EmbodiedCoder: Parameterized Embodied Mobile Manipulation via Modern Coding Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a>. Our method matches or surpasses trained VLAs without <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">additional training</span>.
Tasks involving delicate flow control (e.g., pouring) show a clear advantage. This outcome is aligned with the method: the parameterization stage exposes stable surface normals, contact lines, and symmetry axes, and the trajectory code can explicitly encode motion constraints such as tilt angle limits and collision margins. By grounding semantics in the metric scene through parameterized geometry, the system avoids the ambiguity that can arise when control is derived only from learned visual features.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We also compare to code-generation approaches that predict constraint points or action scripts such as ReKep <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>, VoxPoser <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, and Code-as-Monitor <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="TABLE III ‣ IV-C Simple Task Evaluation ‣ IV EXPERIMENT ‣ EmbodiedCoder: Parameterized Embodied Mobile Manipulation via Modern Coding Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>.
Our method attains consistently higher success.
Unlike other approaches that infer control points or directly output trajectories, EmbodiedCoder first fits parameterized representations, which simplifies following trajectory planning and improves task success rate and stability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">IV-D</span> </span><span id="S4.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Ablation Study</span></h3>

<section id="S4.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS4.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-D</span>1 </span>Effect of Object Shape</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We assess grasping across objects with distinct morphology (bottles, oranges, plastic bags) and compare with AnyGrasp <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> (Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="TABLE V ‣ IV-D1 Effect of Object Shape ‣ IV-D Ablation Study ‣ IV EXPERIMENT ‣ EmbodiedCoder: Parameterized Embodied Mobile Manipulation via Modern Coding Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE V: </span>Comparison with AnyGrasp <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> on grasp success rates (%) across different objects over 20 trials.</figcaption>
